Before working through the next few chapters, it helps to see the whole map at once. **quanteda** has three basic types of objects, and almost everything you do with the package involves moving between them.

A [corpus](/basic-operations/corpus) saves character strings and document-level variables together in a data frame, combining each text with the information attached to it. A [tokens](/basic-operations/tokens) object instead stores each text as a sequence of tokens. Tokens are less compact than plain character strings, but they keep track of where each word sits in the original text. That positional information is what functions such as `textstat_collocations()`, `tokens_ngrams()`, `tokens_select()` and `fcm()` with its `window` argument use for string-of-words analysis. A [document-feature matrix](/basic-operations/dfm), or DFM, represents documents and their feature frequencies as a matrix. A DFM is the most efficient of the three structures, but no longer records where in a document each word appeared. That is why the many `textstat_*` and `textmodel_*` functions for non-positional, bag-of-words analysis take a DFM as their starting point. For a broader overview of how this workflow fits into quantitative text analysis as a field, see @benoit2020.

Text analysis with **quanteda** goes through all three object types, either explicitly, when you create them yourself one step at a time, or implicitly, when a function creates them behind the scenes. The diagram below shows how they connect: raw text files and any accompanying variables (such as the date or author of a document) become a corpus, a corpus becomes tokens, and tokens become a DFM.

{{<mermaid align="left">}}
```{=markdown}
graph TD
D[Text files]
V[Document-level variables]
C(Corpus)
T(Tokens)
AP["Positional analysis (string-of-words)"]
AN["Non-positional analysis (bag-of-words)"]
M(DFM)
style C stroke-width:4px
style T stroke-width:4px
style M stroke-width:4px
D --> C
V --> C
C --> T
T --> M
T -.-> AP
M -.-> AN
```
{{< /mermaid >}}

For example, if character vectors are given to `dfm()`, it internally constructs corpus and tokens objects before creating a DFM.

## Preprocess tokens, not DFMs

Many operations in **quanteda** exist in two versions, one for tokens and one for DFMs, such as `tokens_select()` and `dfm_select()`, `tokens_lookup()` and `dfm_lookup()`, or `tokens_tolower()` and `dfm_tolower()`. We recommend doing most of your preprocessing on the tokens object, before you create a DFM. Only a tokens object still records the order of words, so only the `tokens_*()` functions can match multi-word expressions such as "New York", keep or remove words within a `window` around a keyword, and leave placeholders with `padding = TRUE` so that removing words does not change the distance between the remaining ones.

A DFM only records how often each feature occurs in each document. Once you have created it, the positional information is gone, and the `dfm_*()` functions treat every feature on its own. They are most useful for steps that depend on frequencies, such as removing rare or very common features with `dfm_trim()`, and for adjusting a DFM you already have.

{{% notice info %}}
For large corpora, **quanteda** also offers `tokens_xptr` objects, which you create with `as.tokens_xptr()`. A `tokens_xptr` object is passed by reference: `tokens_*()` functions modify it directly instead of creating a modified copy of the whole object, which makes preprocessing faster and saves memory. You can pass a `tokens_xptr` object to `dfm()` like an ordinary tokens object, or convert it back with `as.tokens()`. Because the object is modified in place, every variable that refers to it changes too, so call `as.tokens_xptr()` on it again if you need an independent copy.
{{% /notice %}}

